`phpz.Stream` wraps PHP streams for files, stream wrappers, directories and existing stream resources. Operations follow the underlying PHP stream's blocking behavior. The API provides no asynchronous scheduler.

# [Borrowing a PHP stream]($heading.id('borrow-stream'))

This Zig handler reads up to `length` bytes and returns the newly allocated Zend string to PHP:

```zig
pub fn readStream(stream: *phpz.Stream, length: usize) !*phpz.zend.String {
    return stream.readToStr(length);
}
```

Declare resource parameters in a stub comment. PHP has no native `resource` parameter type:

```php
/** @param resource $stream */
function readStream($stream, int $length): string {}
```

Call it from PHP:

```php
$stream = fopen('php://memory', 'w+b');
fwrite($stream, 'hello');
rewind($stream);
echo readStream($stream, 3); // hel
fclose($stream);
```

A `*Stream` parameter accepts regular and persistent stream resources. Keep the resource alive and open while borrowed. The handler must leave the caller's stream open. The equivalent manual specification is `.resource = .{ .as = .stream }`, with optional `optional` or `nullable` flags. `Stream.fromResource()` checks and borrows an existing `*zend.Resource` and sets a PHP `TypeError` on a mismatch.

# [Opening a stream and transferring it to PHP]($heading.id('open-and-transfer'))

```zig
pub fn openMemory(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
    try ctx.call.expectNoArgs();
    const stream = try phpz.Stream.open("php://memory", "w+b", .{});
    stream.toZval(ctx.retval);
}
```

Declare the function in the stub:

```php
/** @return resource */
function openMemory() {}
```

`open()` returns one owned request resource reference. `toZval()` transfers it into an `undef` or `null` destination without incrementing its reference count. After transfer, do not close or transfer that same reference again. Handlers cannot directly return `*Stream`.

`open(path, mode, options)` requires a NUL-terminated path and mode and takes no explicit stream context. Its main options are:

| Option | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `use_path` | Search PHP's `include_path` |
| `ignore_url` | Skip URL wrapper lookup and treat the path as a local filename |
| `report_errors` | Allow PHP to report open failures |
| `must_seek` | Request a seekable read stream. PHP may return a temporary copy whose writes do not reach the original stream |
| `persistent` | Request a new or reused persistent stream, if supported by the wrapper |

All options default to false. Failure returns `OpenFailed`. PHP warnings, exceptions and bailouts follow the engine's rules.

# [Reading, writing and seeking]($heading.id('operations'))

| API | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| `read(buffer)` | Return bytes actually read. Zero can mean EOF or temporarily unavailable data |
| `readToStr(len)` | Return an owned `*zend.String` to release or transfer to PHP. EOF yields an empty string |
| `getLine(buffer)` | Borrow a slice of the buffer, retaining the newline and reserving a byte for NUL. Null can indicate no data, failure or insufficient space |
| `write(bytes)` | Return bytes actually written. Short and zero-length writes are possible |
| `copyTo(destination, limit, &copied)` | Borrow both streams. A null limit copies until EOF or no data is available. After failure the count is not a reliable resume offset |
| `seek(offset, .start/.current/.end)` / `rewind()` | Seek or return an error if unsupported |
| `tell()` / `eof()` | Query the position and EOF state tracked by PHP |
| `flush()` | Flush stream buffers. Durability on disk is separate |
| `truncate(size)` / `sync(data_only)` | Resize or request filesystem synchronization if supported. Flush separately before syncing |

For complete writes, handle short writes and decide when a zero-progress result should trigger a retry or failure. Calling a PHP user stream wrapper may raise an exception or bail out even when the Zig method has an error return.

# [Stream options and locking]($heading.id('stream-options'))

`supportsSync()`, `supportsTruncate()` and `supportsLock()` query current capabilities. The operation itself can still fail. Common options include:

| API | Behavior and errors |
| --- | --- |
| `lock(mode, non_blocking)` / `unlock()` | Use `LockMode.shared` or `.exclusive`. Unsupported locking returns `UnsupportedOperation`, nonblocking contention may return `WouldBlock` and other failures return `LockFailed` |
| `setBlocking(blocking)` | Set blocking mode. Unsupported wrappers return `UnsupportedOperation` |
| `setReadTimeout(seconds, microseconds)` | Set a read timeout. Microseconds must be below 1,000,000 and seconds must fit the target `timeval` |
| `setReadBuffer(size)` / `setWriteBuffer(size)` | Zero disables buffering. A nonzero size requests full buffering and the wrapper may refuse |
| `setChunkSize(size)` | Set the internal chunk size from 1 through `INT_MAX` and return the previous size |
| `stat()` | Return `Stream.Stat` (`zend_stat_t`) without changing position, or return `StatFailed` |

Depending on the option, failures include `UnsupportedOperation`, `SetOptionFailed`, `InvalidTimeout`, `InvalidChunkSize` and `StatFailed`. For example, `php://memory` generally lacks locking, blocking mode and sync. The fallback for read buffering may toggle buffering while ignoring its requested size. Call `flush()` before changing write buffering. Closing the stream releases its locks. `FreeOptions` defaults to no flags. Select `.close` or, for persistent streams, `.close_persistent` when freeing an owned stream.

# [Directories]($heading.id('directories'))

`Stream.openDir(path, .{})` returns an owned directory stream. Call `close()` when finished. Use `readDir(&buffer)` only on directory streams:

```zig
// Inside a function with an open directory stream.
var buffer: phpz.Stream.DirBuffer = undefined;
while (stream.readDir(&buffer)) |name| {
    _ = phpz.printf("%s\n", .{name.ptr});
}
```

Names borrow the caller's buffer until the next read. Copy them for later use. Null denotes either end of directory or a read failure. Entries can include `.` and `..`. Use `rewind()` to start again.

# [Closing streams and working with persistent streams]($heading.id('ownership'))

| Source or operation | Ownership and cleanup |
| --- | --- |
| Argument parsing, `fromResource` or `from` | Borrowed. Leave closing and transfer to the owner |
| `open` / `openDir` | Owned. Transfer to PHP or close during normal cleanup |
| `fromPersistentId(id)` | Null if absent, error on a type mismatch. Success gives one owned request resource reference |
| `toZval` | Transfer an owned reference to PHP |
| `close` | Consume an owned nonpersistent stream and preserve the PHP return code, including a process stream's exit status |
| `closePersistent` | Consume an owned persistent stream reference and remove the persistent registration, invalidating aliases |
| `free(options)` | Release with PHP's low-level flags. `FreeOptions` enables none by default. Standard close flags include `.close`, `.close_casted` and `.close_persistent`. Use only with known ownership |

Choose the closing operation with `isPersistent()`. Even on a nonzero result, a closed pointer cannot be retried or reused. A persistent stream's request resource reference still belongs to the current request.

`defer close()` runs during normal Zig unwinding and may be skipped by PHP bailout. Closing a PHP stream may itself enter PHP. Guard cleanup callbacks that must avoid PHP cannot use it. See [Bailouts and cleanup]($link.page('advanced/bailout')) for ownership and bailout boundaries.
